C Experimental Details
C.1 Implementation Details
Models All the models that are used throughout the work are based on pre-trained models from I2SB5. All the models are fine-tuned from the ADM [11] ImageNet 256×256 model, hence share the same architecture as well as the specific model hyper-parameter settings.
Inverse problem setting All the forward operators A are used in the same manner as in [26], which are adopted from DDRM [21].
Step size, gradient For both Algorithms 1,2, we use constant step size, but scaled to match the signal ratio of the intermediate reconstructions ˆx0|i: ρi = (1 − α2
i|i+1)c, where c is some constant. For SR and deblurring tasks, we take c = 1.0. For JPEG restoration, we take c = 0.5.
When implementing the gradient computation for CDDB, we do not explicitly compute A⊤, but rely on automatic differentiation for simplicity. This lets us unify the implementations of Algorithm 1,2 in a similar manner.
Compute All experiments were run using a single RTX 3090 GPU. On average, I2SB and CDDB with 1000 NFE take about 82 seconds (∼ 0.08 sec. / iter). CDDB-deep takes about 193 seconds (∼ 0.19 sec. / iter).
Code Availability Code is available at https://github.com/HJ-harry/CDDB
C.2 Comparison Methods
I2SB We follow the default setting advised in [26] and set the default sampling schedule to be the quadratic schedule [35] with 1000 NFE. As we leverage the pre-trained checkpoints provided, deblurring and inpainting models are set to OT-ODE with no additive Gaussian noise during the sampling process.
DPS, ΠGDM Pre-trained ADM models that were used in the original work [5, 36] are used. For DPS, we use the constant step size of 1.0 with 1000 NFE. For ΠGDM , we use the constant step size of 1.0 with 100 NFE. We initially experimented with higher NFE for ΠGDM but did not find a boost in performance.
DDRM We use 20 NFE DDIM sampling with η = 0.85, ηb = 1.0 as advised.
DDNM We use 100 NFE sampling with η = 0.85 as advised. We do not use time travel for all experiments.
